-- js-env-api.rofl — ATTRIBUTING THE RESIDUE THE CALL GRAPH CANNOT RESOLVE.
--
-- `stdlib_member[audit](C, P, Key)` names a member call whose receiver has a
-- known PROTOTYPE and whose method is not a node in this program: `array.join`,
-- `regexp.test`, `string.concat`, `bigint.toString`. Naming it was
-- w_prototype_of_a_value. Saying WHICH method it is, and SINCE WHEN, is
-- w_env_api_surface — and the facts come from facts/js-lib-surface.rofl, which
-- scanners/ts_lib.ts generates from TypeScript's own `lib.es*.d.ts`.
--
-- THIS PACK NEEDS TWO WORLDS THAT ARE NEVER LOADED TOGETHER, and that is the
-- finding rather than an inconvenience. `stdlib_member` is derived in the
-- CALL-GRAPH world (test/js-corpus-world.ts: js-kinds, js-callgraph, js-dataflow,
-- js-modules, js-shapes, js-statements, js-controlflow) and `environment` /
-- `env_rank` are declared in facts/js-env.rofl, which that world does not load;
-- the ERA world in turn has no call graph. Each half has been complete for
-- days and no world could ask the question.
--
-- The rules are written once and each world derives what it can: the first two
-- need only the call graph, and `lib_unsupported[audit]` needs both, so a pack
-- loaded without facts/js-env.rofl will report `environment` through
-- `undefined_premise[audit]` — which is the kernel saying "you brought half a
-- question", and is the right thing for it to say.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE ATTRIBUTION. One join, and it is one join because both halves already
-- existed: the residue is keyed by (prototype, key) and so is the library.
lib_call[code](C, P, Key, Rel) :- stdlib_member[audit](C, P, Key),
                                  lib_member(P, Key, Rel).

-- ...AND WHAT IT DOES NOT ATTRIBUTE, which is the row worth watching. A member
-- call on a KNOWN prototype whose name TypeScript's declarations do not carry
-- is one of three things and the audit does not guess which: a method of a
-- newer edition than this TypeScript ships, a name the model gave the wrong
-- prototype, or a genuine typo in the corpus. It is empty today and it is the
-- only thing that would notice `prototype_of` becoming wrong.
stdlib_unattributed[audit](C, P, Key) :- stdlib_member[audit](C, P, Key),
                                         not lib_member(P, Key, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE ERA QUESTION, and it is the same question `unsupported[audit]` asks of
-- syntax, on the same scale and in the same years. `Array.prototype.at` is
-- declared in lib.es2022.array.d.ts, so a corpus that calls it is not valid
-- under es2020 — and until now the model could say that about `??=` and not
-- about `.at`, which is the half of the environment question the item's own
-- note calls "what syntax cannot answer".
-- BY COMPOSITION SINCE 2026-09-08, like everything else on this axis. This read
-- `env_rank(E, R), Since > R` while `Since` was a YEAR; it is a RELEASE now —
-- `lib.es2022.array.d.ts` names `es2022` and the file name was always the
-- release rather than the number — so the question is membership and not
-- comparison: does this environment REACH the release that introduced the
-- method. Which is the same question `has_feature` asks of a syntax feature,
-- in the same words, for the first time.
lib_unsupported[audit](E, C, P, Key) :- lib_call[code](C, P, Key, Rel),
                                        environment(E), not reaches[audit](E, Rel).
